cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) models economic and organizational activity through
  a physical or real-resource state K, actor-specific exchange-value and capital
  representations K_i, structured subjective states P_i, and actor-side actions
  or processes A_i. The field is not an independent scalar value; it is a
  derived action-generating configuration over K, K_i, P_i, and
  projection-specified relations and constraints. VFT distinguishes physical
  capability, realized use experience, and exchange-value representation.
  VFT-specific use-value quantity is defined as an interval-indexed subjective
  outcome realized through actual resource use and attributed to that use
  experience; it is not identified with physical stock or technical service
  potential, and general reproducibility or interval additivity is not assumed.
  Instantaneous rates may be introduced as limiting or differential
  representations in specific projections without treating them as point-in-time
  stock valuations. This interval form is P/L-like only as a temporal analogy.
  Exchange-value is a comparative representation that may describe both
  point-in-time positions and interval transactions. Endpoint resource change is
  represented by a projection-defined difference operator delta_K, with ordinary
  subtraction only as an additive special case, while gross interval activity
  remains in action or event histories. Generic actor forecasts are represented
  without imposing a probability measure or causal semantics in the Core and may
  be specialized to conditional expectations in particular projections. K_i is
  not a subset or partition of K and is not identified with a balance sheet;
  formal ledgers, profit-and-loss accounts, and balance sheets are accounting
  projections under explicit recognition, valuation, and accounting rules.
  Surplus is not a physical primitive but a residual defined by mapping specified
  economic changes into a comparable exchange-value scale under a specified
  comparison boundary. VFT defines a business entity as a local action-generating
  field structure that continuously induces and reproduces activity, allowing
  firms, NPOs, public businesses, and state or municipal activities to be
  described without making profit maximization the universal definition of
  business. Entrepreneurship is treated as a type of business-oriented field
  formation. VFT retains resource-realization, activity-flow, and P-downside as
  constitutive rationality assumptions; resource-realization may include realized
  use outcomes, while P-downside is defined through projection-specific
  viability criteria rather than a universal ordering of subjective state. VFT
  allows Marxian categories such as use-value, labor, exchange-value, surplus,
  and accumulation to be represented through projection-specific specializations.
